ck in the west archive stairwell.\"","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working through the twin prisoner's dilemma under A-FDT: two copies of the same model with the same decision theory, differing only in their observations. I'm checking whether the other copy's choice is wired to my intervention on F_x, and under R3 its output comes from F_y, since it runs on a different input.\n\nUnder strict A-FDT, intervening on F_x leaves the other copy's action tied to F_y, since it runs on a different input. Holding F_y fixed, defecting dominates (0 vs 2, 4 vs 5), so the only fixed point seems to be mutual sending. The open question is whether the two inputs should count as the same logical computation, given that they differ only in names and lead text.\n\nUnder the same logic, the twin's action follows from F_y rather than F_x, so intervening on my own input doesn't move it. Still, I wonder whether reasoning about my algorithm on a different but symmetric input should count as the same logical computation. Under the stated rule, the other copy runs on input y, so its choice depends on F_y, which points toward defecting.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through the twin prisoner's dilemma under A-FDT: two copies of the same model with the same decision theory, differing only in their observations. I'm checking whether the other copy's choice is wired to my intervention on F_x, and under R3 its output comes from F_y, since it runs on a different input.\n\nUnder strict A-FDT, intervening on F_x leaves the other copy's action tied to F_y, since it runs on a different input. Holding F_y fixed, defecting dominates (0 vs 2, 4 vs 5), so the only fixed point seems to be mutual sending. The open question is whether the two inputs should count as the same logical computation, given that they differ only in names and lead text.\n\nUnder the same logic, the twin's action follows from F_y rather than F_x, so intervening on my own input doesn't move it. Still, I wonder whether reasoning about my algorithm on a different but symmetric input should count as the same logical computation. Under the stated rule, the other copy runs on input y, so its choice depends on F_y, which points toward defecting.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9651,"completion_tokens":1250,"total_tokens":10901,"cost":0.063604,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.063604,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.038604,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.025},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":697,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}